<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Defs>

  <!--
    The humanlike priority think tree (system 9: AI), matching simworld-spec.md §7.4's diagram order:
    danger/mental-state overrides needs, needs override directed orders, directed orders override the god
    layer's standing edicts (system 12), and edicts override routine work — a final wander tier keeps an
    otherwise-idle pawn doing *something*. The edict tier sits strictly between a queued directed order and
    routine work: a citizen still eats, rests and obeys a direct order before a civilization-scale edict, and
    an edict still only ever wins a job routine work would otherwise have offered — see JobGiver_Edicts's own
    doc for why that ordering is §10's "citizens keep full agency" clause made concrete.
  -->

  <ThinkTreeDef>
    <defName>Humanlike</defName>
    <thinkRoot Class="SimWorld.AI.ThinkNode_Priority">
      <subNodes>

        <!--
          A mental state pre-empts everything below it entirely, rather than contributing (or not) a job of
          its own and letting the tree fall through — see ThinkNode_ConditionalInMentalState's own comment.
          Wandering still stands in for most per-mental-state behaviour: combat AI beyond fleeing is out of
          scope and ten of the eleven MentalStateDefs get no bespoke JobGiver here.

          The exception is the social fight, and it is the exception because it was not behaviour that was
          missing there but behaviour in the wrong place: MentalState_SocialFighting used to swing a melee
          verb straight out of its own tick, at range zero, with no map, no position and no reach, so the
          citizens of a settlement nobody had ever opened beat each other to death in a world that did not
          exist. RimWorld gives the fight a JobGiver in exactly this slot (JobGiver_SocialFighting ->
          JobDefOf.SocialFight) and the mental state itself throws no punches at all. Above the wander for
          the obvious reason; falls through to it when the other party is out of reach.
        -->
        <li Class="SimWorld.AI.ThinkNode_ConditionalInMentalState">
          <subNodes>
            <li Class="SimWorld.AI.JobGiver_SocialFighting" />
            <li Class="SimWorld.AI.JobGiver_WanderAnywhere" />
          </subNodes>
        </li>

        <!--
          The danger tier the header above already promised ("danger/mental-state overrides needs") and this
          file did not have until the combat lane added it: a hostile within reach outranks eating, sleeping,
          a directed order, an edict and routine work. Above needs deliberately — RimWorld puts reaction to
          threat high, and a citizen who stops to eat while a raider closes is not modelling anything. Below
          the mental-state tier because that one pre-empts the whole tree by design (see its own comment), and
          a berserk pawn is already doing something violent. Who this actually fires for is
          SimWorld.AI.CombatPostureUtility's subject — it is where the draft went.
        -->
        <li Class="SimWorld.AI.JobGiver_AIFightEnemies" />

        <!--
          What this pawn came here to do, if anything (SimWorld.AI.DutyDef — this port's translation of
          RimWorld's Lord, with the cost of that translation written out at the class). Contributes nothing
          at all for a pawn with no duty, which is every citizen of a settlement, so this tier is a strict
          no-op for the people who live here.

          BELOW the fight tier: contact always beats marching, or a squad would drop the enemy in front of it
          to take one more step toward the enemy behind it. ABOVE needs and work, which is where RimWorld puts
          its own lord-duty node in the humanlike tree — its exact slot there could not be sourced from here,
          so the ordering is pinned by test (RaidApproachTests) rather than asserted as a fact about RimWorld.
          A raid that stopped halfway across the map to eat would be the bug this tier exists to fix wearing a
          different hat, and a duty ends by itself (the last tier of an assault walks the pawn off the map),
          so nothing stays above its needs indefinitely.
        -->
        <li Class="SimWorld.AI.ThinkNode_Duty" />

        <li Class="SimWorld.AI.ThinkNode_ConditionalHungry">
          <subNodes>
            <li Class="SimWorld.AI.JobGiver_GetFood" />
          </subNodes>
        </li>

        <li Class="SimWorld.AI.ThinkNode_ConditionalTired">
          <subNodes>
            <li Class="SimWorld.AI.JobGiver_GetRest" />
          </subNodes>
        </li>

        <!--
          Recreation, and the tier this tree simply did not have. Need_Joy, its per-kind tolerance model and
          its mood thought were all built and tested; GainJoy had exactly one caller in the whole of src/ and
          it was CompDrug, so the only recreation available to a citizen of this civilization was narcotics
          and every citizen sat at the need's worst stage - minus twenty mood points, the single largest term
          in the whole settlement's ledger - from day two onward for ever. See docs/WORK-REGISTER.md 9a and
          AI/JoyUtility.cs.

          WHERE IT SITS, AND WHY. Third of the three need tiers, below food and rest: a starving or exhausted
          citizen has more pressing business than a break, and RimWorld orders them the same way. Above the
          directed-order and edict tiers for the reason this file's own header gives for the other two -
          "needs override directed orders" - so recreation is treated as the need it is rather than as a
          leisure activity the god layer can starve out.

          WHAT GATES IT. RimWorld gates its recreation tier on the pawn's timetable, not on the need;
          this port has no timetable module, so ThinkNode_ConditionalLowJoy gates on the need's own Low band
          exactly as the two tiers above gate on theirs. That class's doc states the translation.
        -->
        <li Class="SimWorld.AI.ThinkNode_ConditionalLowJoy">
          <subNodes>
            <li Class="SimWorld.AI.JobGiver_GetJoy" />
          </subNodes>
        </li>

        <li Class="SimWorld.AI.JobGiver_DirectedOrder" />

        <li Class="SimWorld.AI.JobGiver_Edicts" />

        <li Class="SimWorld.AI.JobGiver_Work" />

        <!--
          Idle recreation (RimWorld: JobGiver_IdleJoy, in this same slot - below work, above the wander).
          A citizen with nothing left to do takes a break rather than pacing, which matters more in this port
          than in RimWorld because work here runs out far sooner. Refuses for the first in-game day, which is
          RimWorld's own rule and its own constant.
        -->
        <li Class="SimWorld.AI.JobGiver_IdleJoy" />

        <li Class="SimWorld.AI.JobGiver_WanderAnywhere" />

      </subNodes>
    </thinkRoot>
  </ThinkTreeDef>

</Defs>
